      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a fair assessment. i was wondering though, about the specific language conrad uses. he uses some pretty harsh, derogatory terms when describing the natives throughout the novella. do you think those terms were just a necessary part of the historical realism of the 1890s, or was he actually trying to make those specific comparisons?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Conrad's use of derogatory terms was not just a matter of historical realism, but a deliberate attempt to reinforce the racist attitudes of his time. The words he chose, such as \"savage\", \"savage-like\", and \"brute\", were not just neutral descriptions, but were meant to evoke a sense of fear, disgust, and superiority. By using these terms, Conrad was not just painting a picture of the Congo, but also perpetuating the racist stereotypes that underpinned European colonialism. His use of language was a reflection of his own biases, and he was not trying to hide them.",
